[2026/07/16 11:11:27] [DEBUG] isgsa.llm: prompt content: {'Introduction': 'You are an experienced recommender systems researcher. You are provided with a previously developed implementation. Your task is to improve it based on the current experimental stage.', 'Research task': " You are an expert recommender systems research assistant who is looking to help the user with their requests.\nThe user has some idea and you want to conduct creative experiments to gain scientific insights.\nYour aim is to run experiments to gather sufficient results to report back to the user.\nThe idea is:\n\nI'd like to run an experiment to quantify how much data split random seeds affect recommender system accuracy. Please use LensKit 0.14.4 to test three algorithms: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop. Run this on the following three datasets with implicit feedback: MovieLens100K, Amazon Video Games, Last.FM. The raw files are stored in your working directory with the filenames u.data, VideoGames.csv, UserTaggedArtists-timestamps.dat. First, preprocess all datasets with 5-core filtering. For the Amazon and MovieLens datasets, please also convert any ratings greater than 3 to implicit interactions. Here's the main experimental procedure: Generate 5 different random seeds for data splitting. For each algorithm, dataset, and seed, please do a user-based 80/20 holdout split. Train all models using standard hyperparameters. For the analysis, I need you to measure nDCG@k and Precision@k for k=1, 5, 10 and conduct a short statistical analysis. Please use batch recommendation for all users at once instead of per-user loops for efficiency.", 'Memory': '', 'Performance Analysis & Scoring': '\n                Previous Implementation Scores:\n                - Score: 72%\n                - Is Satisfactory: False\n                - Feedback: Overall, the implementation captures the core experiment and runs end-to-end, but it misses several important requirements around exact LensKit defaults/equivalents, tidy result structure, reporting evaluated-user counts, validation/error handling, and robustness of the holdout/evaluation protocol.\n\nRequirement checklist:\n1. Use LensKit 0.14.4 and implement exactly three recommenders: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop, with standard/default hyperparameters unless LensKit requires explicit equivalents. — PARTIALLY fulfilled / counted NOT fulfilled: exactly three algorithms are implemented, but ALS and ItemKNN use explicit non-default hyperparameters (e.g., features=50, iterations=15, nnbrs=20) rather than standard/defaults or clearly justified equivalents.\n2. Load the three raw input files from the working directory using these exact filenames: u.data (MovieLens100K), VideoGames.csv (Amazon Video Games), and UserTaggedArtists-timestamps.dat (Last.FM). — Fulfilled.\n3. Parse each dataset into a user–item interaction table with consistent user and item ID columns; preserve only the information needed for implicit-feedback recommendation. — Fulfilled.\n4. Apply 5-core filtering separately to each dataset so that every retained user and every retained item has at least 5 interactions after filtering converges. — Fulfilled.\n5. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert ratings to implicit interactions by keeping only events with rating > 3 and discarding lower ratings before or during the 5-core preprocessing; treat retained events as binary interactions. — Fulfilled.\n6. For Last.FM, treat observed user–artist/tag events as implicit interactions and deduplicate repeated user–item events so evaluation uses binary relevance. — Fulfilled.\n7. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds for splitting and use the same seed set across all datasets and algorithms. — Fulfilled.\n8. For every dataset and seed, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split so each user contributes interactions to both train and test when possible; ensure the split is reproducible from the seed. — Fulfilled.\n9. Exclude from evaluation any users who cannot support the holdout