sult structure, reporting evaluated-user counts, validation/error handling, and robustness of the holdout/evaluation protocol.\n\nRequirement checklist:\n1. Use LensKit 0.14.4 and implement exactly three recommenders: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop, with standard/default hyperparameters unless LensKit requires explicit equivalents. — PARTIALLY fulfilled / counted NOT fulfilled: exactly three algorithms are implemented, but ALS and ItemKNN use explicit non-default hyperparameters (e.g., features=50, iterations=15, nnbrs=20) rather than standard/defaults or clearly justified equivalents.\n2. Load the three raw input files from the working directory using these exact filenames: u.data (MovieLens100K), VideoGames.csv (Amazon Video Games), and UserTaggedArtists-timestamps.dat (Last.FM). — Fulfilled.\n3. Parse each dataset into a user–item interaction table with consistent user and item ID columns; preserve only the information needed for implicit-feedback recommendation. — Fulfilled.\n4. Apply 5-core filtering separately to each dataset so that every retained user and every retained item has at least 5 interactions after filtering converges. — Fulfilled.\n5. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert ratings to implicit interactions by keeping only events with rating > 3 and discarding lower ratings before or during the 5-core preprocessing; treat retained events as binary interactions. — Fulfilled.\n6. For Last.FM, treat observed user–artist/tag events as implicit interactions and deduplicate repeated user–item events so evaluation uses binary relevance. — Fulfilled.\n7. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds for splitting and use the same seed set across all datasets and algorithms. — Fulfilled.\n8. For every dataset and seed, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split so each user contributes interactions to both train and test when possible; ensure the split is reproducible from the seed. — Fulfilled.\n9. Exclude from evaluation any users who cannot support the holdout